    "content": "「可持續發展」包含環境、經濟與社會三個面向。試以一項具體政策為例，說明三者之間如何可能出現取捨，並指出處理此取捨時應考慮甚麼。",
    "explanation": "本題要求以具體政策承載分析，泛論三者關係而無實例，論述無從檢驗。第三問須提出判斷準則，而非重複「要平衡」。",
    "referenceAnswer": "以「限制高排放車輛進入市區」為例。\n環境面向：市區空氣質素改善，懸浮粒子與氮氧化物濃度下降，呼吸道疾病風險降低。此為政策的主要目標。\n經濟面向：以舊款貨車營運的小型物流商須提早更換車輛，資本開支上升；部分營運成本會轉嫁至貨品價格。短期內業界收入受壓。\n社會面向：受影響最大的往往是議價能力較弱的自僱司機與小商戶，他們的儲備有限，難以承擔一次性的更換成本。若無配套，政策的代價會集中落在承受能力最低的一群。\n取捨的所在：\n環境效益由全體市民共享，而轉型成本由特定群體承擔。這是典型的「收益分散、成本集中」結構——正因成本集中，受影響者的反對強度遠高於受惠者的支持強度，政策阻力往往大於其淨效益所應引起的程度。\n處理取捨時應考慮：\n一、成本與效益的分佈，而非只看總量。總量為正不代表安排公平。\n二、是否設有過渡安排，例如分階段實施、資助換車、提供低息貸款，使承受能力最低者不被迫即時退出市場。\n三、是否存在達致同一環境目標而分配後果較輕的替代手段。\n四、可逆性：若政策效果不如預期，能否調整。",
    "marks": 1,
    "referenceAnswerEn": "Take a restriction on high-emission vehicles entering the urban core.\nEnvironmental dimension: urban air quality improves, concentrations of particulates and nitrogen oxides fall, and respiratory risk declines. This is the policy's main objective.\nEconomic dimension: small logistics operators running older trucks must replace vehicles sooner, raising capital outlay, and part of the higher operating cost passes into the price of goods. Industry incomes are squeezed in the short run.\nSocial dimension: those most affected are often self-employed drivers and small traders with weak bargaining power and thin reserves, who cannot readily meet a one-off replacement cost. Without accompanying measures the cost of the policy falls on those least able to bear it.\nWhere the conflict lies: the environmental benefit is shared by all residents while the cost of transition falls on a specific group. This is the familiar structure of dispersed benefits and concentrated costs, and because the costs are concentrated, opposition from those affected is far stronger than support from those who gain, so resistance often exceeds what the net benefit alone would suggest.\nWhat should be considered: first, the distribution of costs and benefits and not only their totals — a positive total does not make the arrangement fair. Second, whether transitional arrangements exist, such as phased implementation, replacement subsidies or low-interest loans, so that the least able are not forced out of the market at once. Third, whether an alternative means exists that reaches the same environmental objective with milder distributional consequences. Fourth, reversibility: whether the policy can be adjusted if its effects fall short.",
    "contentEn": "Sustainable development has environmental, economic and social dimensions. Using one concrete policy as an example, show how the three may come into conflict, and state what should be considered in resolving the conflict.",
